 "text": "Everyone remembers the 300 Spartans, but they weren't alone. 480 BC. Persian King Xerxes invades Greece with a massive army. About 7,000 Greeks block a narrow mountain pass called Thermopylae. Only 300 of them are Spartans, led by King Leonidas. For three days, the pass holds. Then a local man named Ephialtes shows the Persians a secret path around the mountains, the Greeks are about to be surrounded. Leonidas sends most of the army away, but the Spartans stay, and so do about 700 soldiers from a small city called Thespiae. They fight to the very end. History mostly remembers the Spartans, a defeat that became the most famous last stand in history. Every legend has a beginning.",
